<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Mosquito Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1625 CE – 1860 CE (approx.) · capital Bluefields</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Miskito kingdom on the Caribbean coast of Nicaragua and Honduras, allied with Britain, which kept Spain out of the coast for two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miskito">Miskito people</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast, who absorbed Africans from shipwrecks and runaways in the 17th century, formed close ties with English traders and buccaneers. By the mid-17th century their leaders were recognised by the English as kings, a title confirmed through the governors of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miskito">Miskito</a> kings were often crowned under British auspices in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a> or <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/belize">Belize</a> and relied on British arms and trade. Real power was shared with regional governors, generals and admirals. Miskito warriors raided Spanish settlements and other indigenous groups, taking captives for sale. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> maintained the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mosquito-shore">Mosquito Shore</a> settlement from 1749 to 1787 and later declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bluefields">Bluefields</a> as the centre.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-managua">Treaty of Managua</a> of 1860 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> recognised Nicaraguan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and the kingdom became the self-governing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mosquito-reserve">Mosquito Reserve</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miskito">Miskito</a> remain the main people of the coast.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mosquito-kingdom.jpg" alt="Mosquito Kingdom" width="320" height="250"><figcaption>Image: Rowanwindwhistler; derivative work by Rowanwindwhistler · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_Miskito-es.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1625 CE – 1860 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bluefields</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jeremy I, Robert Charles Frederic, George Augustus Frederic II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Miskito religion, later Moravian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Miskito</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>, <a href="/countries/honduras">Honduras</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Miskito Kingdom, Mosquito Coast, Kingdom of Mosquitia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1742&amp;focus=mosquito-kingdom" aria-label="Mosquito Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2378 1730 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mosquito Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2698 1986L4997 651M2698 1986L2698 1986"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M2698 1986h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2698 1986h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2698 1986h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bluefields. <a href="/atlas?year=1742&amp;focus=mosquito-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
